WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.519
Part two of the SimplyFile showcase: ten more situations a practice meets every season - last year's returns and how they are put right, and this year's harder cases. Every client is made up for this demo.

2
00:00:15.125 --> 00:00:31.225
Lakshmi Devi filed her return for assessment year 2025-26 in November 2025 - after the 31st of July due date. Choose the year first; a new client starts in the year on the screen.

3
00:00:41.061 --> 00:00:45.451
Her statement and 26AS go in as usual.

4
00:01:00.765 --> 00:01:14.585
Filed on the 20th of November 2025: SimplyFile calls it a belated return under section 139(4), and works out 234A and 234B to the day she filed.

5
00:01:15.203 --> 00:01:25.903
Suresh Reddy, a trader, filed in July 2026. Later his A I S showed fixed-deposit interest he had not reported.

6
00:01:53.984 --> 00:02:00.004
The original return, filed on the 25th of July 2026.

7
00:02:01.609 --> 00:02:11.189
Before the 31st of December he may revise. Start a revised return and add the missed interest as its own statement.

8
00:02:14.936 --> 00:02:25.326
Sign off and file again: a revised return under section 139(5). The original stays in the history, marked superseded.

9
00:02:27.109 --> 00:02:37.409
Anjali Sharma filed in July 2025. After the 31st of December she found municipal tax she had never claimed.

10
00:03:22.421 --> 00:03:34.281
Only an updated return is possible now - and an ITR-U can only raise tax. Claiming the deduction would lower it, so SimplyFile says no, and why.

11
00:03:48.546 --> 00:03:54.286
Discard the revision. The original return stands, as filed.

12
00:03:54.890 --> 00:04:05.880
Vijay Kumar has a business and two lakh fifty thousand of deductions under 80C, 80D and 80CCD(1B). Which regime is better?

13
00:04:39.984 --> 00:04:55.924
Switch the regime and both computations are there: the old regime, with the deductions, is two lakh twenty-six thousand two hundred - the new regime is one lakh twenty-four thousand eight hundred. The new regime saves him over a lakh.

14
00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:02.910
Dr Harish Menon earns one point two crore from his practice, with a tax audit.

15
00:05:47.405 --> 00:06:00.195
Surcharge at fifteen percent, with the marginal-relief check: tax thirty-nine lakh eighty-two thousand. TDS and four advance-tax instalments leave a small refund.

16
00:06:00.811 --> 00:06:10.531
Arjun Pillai lives in Dubai and earns rent and N R O interest in India, taxed at thirty percent at source. Tick non-resident.

17
00:06:48.250 --> 00:07:02.430
A non-resident gets no rebate under 87A, so there is a little tax - nine thousand two hundred and sixty - and over two lakh of excess TDS comes back as a refund, with 244A interest.

18
00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:14.530
Greenfield Agritech is an L L P without audit. Its due date was the 31st of August 2026; it filed on the 10th of September.

19
00:08:10.217 --> 00:08:20.537
Taxed at thirty percent like a firm. Filed late, it is a belated return with 234A, B and C worked out to the filing date.

20
00:08:21.155 --> 00:08:27.755
Prakash Joshi pays three lakh fifty thousand of interest on the loan for the home he lives in.

21
00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:12.380
Under the old regime the house-property loss is capped at two lakh. With 80C his taxable income is eight lakh fifty thousand, and tax eighty-five thousand eight hundred.

22
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:20.029
Fathima Begum's books claim eight thousand of TDS that her 26AS does not show.

23
00:09:53.234 --> 00:10:01.564
Left unexplained, the red keeps the year in Reconciling - the return cannot be signed off until someone looks into it.

24
00:10:02.155 --> 00:10:11.135
Gopal Krishna filed on time for assessment year 2025-26 - but the acknowledgement number was typed wrong.

25
00:10:53.500 --> 00:11:03.240
Undo 'filed' with a reason, then file again with the right number. Nothing is deleted - both entries stay in the history.

26
00:11:07.765 --> 00:11:19.595
Firm analytics now shows the firm's season across all fifteen clients: returns filed and on time, tax handled, refunds secured, and the interest clients paid.

27
00:11:20.186 --> 00:11:25.586
The deadline radar lists every return still open, overdue first.

28
00:11:26.203 --> 00:11:42.663
And the growth opportunities: advance-tax planning for those who paid 234B and C, refunds that show too much TDS, late filers, revised returns, open mismatches - and last year's clients not yet carried into this year.

29
00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:51.390
That is part two. Part three shows how last years' returns come into SimplyFile in a minute each.
